{"content": "Public-key cryptography (also known as asymmetric cryptography) allows two strangers to agree on a secret without ever meeting in person or sharing a secret key over an insecure channel.\n\nThe core mechanism relies on **mathematical one-way functions**: operations that are easy to perform in one direction but computationally difficult to reverse.\n\nHere is the step-by-step logical deduction of how this works, using the most common standard (RSA) and the Diffie-Hellman key exchange as conceptual models.\n\n### 1. The Fundamental Concept: The Lock and Key Analogy\nIn symmetric cryptography (like a padlock), the sender and receiver must share the same key. If they are strangers, they must first agree on this key, which is the \"Key Distribution Problem.\"\n\nPublic-key cryptography solves this", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "Public", "logprob": -0.9042100740763175, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "Public", "logprob": -0.9042100740763175}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -1.8582277132364737}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -2.294075949320458}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -2.3719510866495597}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -2.935627920756005}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -3.0197477175089347}, {"token": "This", "logprob": -3.4208440615030753}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -3.6313810182901847}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -4.5199727846476065}, {"token": "Actually", "logprob": -4.661652548441552}, {"token": "How", "logprob": -4.704615576395653}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -4.778098089823388}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -4.799603445658349}, {"token": "You", "logprob": -5.217520697245263}, {"token": "Strict", "logprob": -5.5851583315226065}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -5.8151693178507315}, {"token": "I", "logprob": -5.900694830546044}, {"token": "When", "logprob": -6.395243628153466}, {"token": "Let", "logprob": -6.407206518778466}, {"token": "While", "logprob": -6.444939596781396}]}, {"token": "-key", "logprob": -0.004759245330063777, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "