{"content": "The main differences between **TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)** and **UDP (User Datagram Protocol)** lie in their design goals, reliability, speed, and use cases. Both are transport layer protocols in the OSI model, but they serve different purposes.\n\nHere\u2019s a detailed comparison:\n\n### 1. Connection-Oriented vs. Connectionless\n- **TCP**: **Connection-oriented**. It establishes a three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) before data transfer begins. This ensures both parties are ready to communicate.\n- **UDP**: **Connectionless**. No handshake is required. Data packets (datagrams) are sent immediately without establishing a connection.\n\n### 2. Reliability\n- **TCP**: **Reliable**. It guarantees:\n  - **Delivery", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "The", "logprob": -0.5620023886602006, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "The", "logprob": -0.5620023886602006}, {"token": "TCP", "logprob": -1.0572034995000443}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -3.759567467395552}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -4.63528463048149}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -4.7846185843389115}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -4.7998983542363725}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -4.809747902576216}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -5.260187355701216}, {"token": "While", "logprob": -5.406748024646529}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -6.122411934558638}, {"token": "When", "logprob": -6.2806818167607865}, {"token": "There", "logprob": -6.304224220935591}, {"token": "Transport", "logprob": -6.97363683385063}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -7.270673958484419}, {"token": "Transmission", "logprob": -7.346923081104048}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -7.940289704028853}, {"token": "Both", "logprob": -7.952798096362837}, {"token": "Since", "logprob": -8.188522545520552}, {"token": "Network", "logprob": -8.232528893176802}, {"token": "I", "logprob": -8.239259926501997}]}, {"token": " main", "logprob": -0.10777206349829882, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " main", "logprob": -0.10777206349829882}, {"token": " primary", "