{"content": "A **Merkle tree** (also known as a hash tree) is a data structure used in computer science, particularly in cryptography and distributed systems. It is a tree-like structure where each non-leaf node is labeled with the cryptographic hash of the concatenation of its child nodes.\n\n### How It Works\n1. **Leaf Nodes**: These contain the data (e.g., transactions, files, or blocks). Each leaf is hashed to produce a hash value.\n2. **Parent Nodes**: Each parent node is the hash of the concatenated hashes of its two children.\n3. **Root Node**: The topmost node is called the **Merkle root**. It represents the entire dataset.\n\nThis structure allows for efficient and secure verification of data integrity.\n\n### Key Properties", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "A", "logprob": -0.4568993910336516, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "A", "logprob": -0.4568993910336516}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -1.5705563887143157}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -2.6405141218686126}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -2.8219086988949798}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -3.9226014479184173}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -5.8819596632504485}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -6.53089307389498}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -6.812888847210409}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -7.20472119889498}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -7.888957725384238}, {"token": "What", "logprob": -8.050883994915488}, {"token": "I", "logprob": -8.224061714031699}, {"token": "Since", "logprob": -9.388636337139609}, {"token": "M", "logprob": -9.51738046250582}, {"token": "This", "logprob": -10.008414970257284}, {"token": "Definition", "logprob": -10.102105842449667}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -10.130103813030722}, {"token": "Under", "logprob": -10.15146421036959}, {"token": "As", "logprob": -10.264935241558554}, {"token": "An", "logprob": -10.384149299480917}]}, {"token": " **", "logprob": -0.000833374617243976, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " **", "logprob": -0.000833374617243976}, {"token": " Mer", "logprob": -7.243289810774470