{"content": "# Big-O Notation Explained\n\n## What Is Big-O Notation?\n\nBig-O notation is a mathematical tool used in computer science to describe the **asymptotic upper bound** of an algorithm's time or space complexity. It characterizes how the runtime (or memory usage) of an algorithm grows as the input size increases.\n\nKey points:\n- It focuses on the **worst-case** scenario.\n- It ignores constants and lower-order terms.\n- It describes **growth rate**, not absolute time.\n\nCommon complexities (from fastest to slowest):\n| Notation | Name | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| O(1) | Constant | Array access by index |\n| O(log n) | Logarithmic |", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "#", "logprob": -0.3086533446827069, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "#", "logprob": -0.3086533446827069}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -1.8416771789112225}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -2.951452722124113}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -3.3314347167530194}, {"token": "Big", "logprob": -4.9193348784961834}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -5.242756356767668}, {"token": "I", "logprob": -6.926187982133879}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -7.020040978960051}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -7.15007256465341}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -7.357275476030363}, {"token": "What", "logprob": -7.439539422563566}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -7.618637551836027}, {"token": "Explanation", "logprob": -8.009866227678312}, {"token": "An", "logprob": -8.362921227983488}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -9.425711144975676}, {"token": "Understanding", "logprob": -9.609773149065031}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -9.727245797685637}, {"token": "As", "logprob": -9.777995576433195}, {"token": "Ex", "logprob": -9.787042130998625}, {"token": "Think", "logprob": -10.146818627885832}]}, {"token": " Big", "logprob": -0.13905040645512656, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " Big", "logprob": -0.13905040645512656}, {"token": " Understanding", "logprob": -2.0712231817236813}, {"token": " Explanation", "logprob": -6.638791007041064}, {"token": " W