{"content": "Vaccines train the immune system by simulating an infection without causing the disease itself. This process allows the body to develop **immunological memory**, enabling it to recognize and fight the specific pathogen much faster and more effectively if it encounters the real virus or bacteria in the future.\n\nHere is a step-by-step breakdown of how this process works:\n\n### 1. Introduction of the Antigen\nA vaccine contains a harmless version of a pathogen (virus or bacteria) or parts of it. These components are called **antigens**. Depending on the type of vaccine, the antigen may be:\n- A weakened (attenuated) live virus.\n- An inactivated (killed) virus.\n- A specific protein subunit of the virus.\n- A", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "V", "logprob": -0.27191064670274656, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "V", "logprob": -0.27191064670274656}, {"token": "To", "logprob": -2.2667226584214966}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -2.887505510594348}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -3.4858484061021606}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -4.33718011691759}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -5.37827012851427}, {"token": "When", "logprob": -5.901587465550403}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -5.959681490208606}, {"token": "Think", "logprob": -6.089047411229114}, {"token": "Vac", "logprob": -6.280386904026965}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -6.465830782200793}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -6.54033181979845}, {"token": "How", "logprob": -6.664980867650012}, {"token": "Modern", "logprob": -6.880457857396106}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -7.048968294407825}, {"token": "Understanding", "logprob": -7.079754808690051}, {"token": "Determin", "logprob": -7.192115762974719}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -7.449526766087512}, {"token": "This", "logprob": -7.4600705893297}, {"token": " Vacc", "logprob": -7.623133638646106}]}, {"token": "acc", "logprob": -0.022809348506463323, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "acc", "logprob": -0.022809348506463323}, {"token": "act", "logprob": -4.690065703792108}, {"