{"content": "To prove that the square root of 2 ($\\sqrt{2}$) is irrational, we will use the method of **proof by contradiction**.\n\n### Step 1: Assumption\nAssume the opposite: that $\\sqrt{2}$ is a **rational** number.\n\nBy definition, a rational number can be expressed as a fraction $\\frac{a}{b}$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers, $b \\neq 0$, and the fraction is in its **simplest form** (i.e., $a$ and $b$ share no common factors other than 1; they are coprime).\n\nSo, let:\n$$ \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{a}{b} $$\n\n### Step 2", "reasoning": null, "logprobs": [{"token": "To", "logprob": -0.01798522822129911, "top_logprobs": [{"token": "To", "logprob": -0.01798522822129911}, {"token": "Here", "logprob": -4.978609923045518}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -5.718903425853135}, {"token": "We", "logprob": -6.04112518183458}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -6.59306609980333}, {"token": "This", "logprob": -6.65840041987169}, {"token": "###", "logprob": -7.24815643183458}, {"token": "There", "logprob": -7.946024779002549}, {"token": "#", "logprob": -8.31672561518419}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -8.731545332591416}, {"token": "Proof", "logprob": -8.797549132029893}, {"token": "I", "logprob": -8.94342124811876}, {"token": "Ass", "logprob": -8.978976133983018}, {"token": "In", "logprob": -9.125838164012315}, {"token": "It", "logprob": -9.678001288096787}, {"token": "##", "logprob": -9.697820547740342}, {"token": "Standard", "logprob": -9.8082627093004}, {"token": "Actually", "logprob": -10.304146650950791}, {"token": "Based", "logprob": -10.764338377635362}, {"token": "One", "logprob": -10.834566000621201}]}, {"token": " prove", "logprob": -9.477255365908377e-05, "top_logprobs": [{"token": " prove", "logprob": -9.477255365908377e-05}, {"token": " demonstrate", "logprob": -10.475921035981393}, {"token": " provide", "logprob": -11.477210403657175}, {"token": " proof", "logprob": -11.662446380830026}, {"token": " determine", "logprob": -11.748629928803659}, {"token": " Prove", "logprob": -12